51
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:21,340
For those who like one that's unstoppable, meet the King of Thrones, the Champion Four.

52
00:03:21,341 --> 00:03:28,540
American Standard makes the bold claim that this titanic of toilets is virtually unclogable.

53
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:33,600
We put it to the test to see what goes down with the ship.

54
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:40,980
Some of the things that go into toilets that really shouldn't are toys and cell phones.

55
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:43,820
Up to 7 million cell phones a year are dropped in the toilet.

56
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:49,760
And so we do consider those things when we're designing, but these are not things that belong in your toilet.

57
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:58,340
Another industry test is called the dye exchange test for the removal of liquid waste.

58
00:03:58,341 --> 00:04:04,340
So far, it passes the test.

59
00:04:04,341 --> 00:04:11,340
And then there's this little experiment using a stand-in for something that's no stranger to being flushed.

60
00:04:11,341 --> 00:04:18,340
Another industry test is to take miso, which is soy paste, and put it in a latex container.

61
00:04:18,341 --> 00:04:20,740
and put it in a latex container.

62
00:04:20,741 --> 00:04:23,720
This container holds 50 grams of miso.

63
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:26,180
Studies show that humans can eliminate

64
00:04:26,181 --> 00:04:29,560
up to 350 grams of solid waste.

65
00:04:29,561 --> 00:04:32,320
So I have 500 grams here in my hand,

66
00:04:32,321 --> 00:04:34,380
and we test toilets like the Champion 4

67
00:04:34,381 --> 00:04:36,160
up to 1,000 grams.

68
00:04:36,161 --> 00:04:38,160
So let's put them in the toilet.

69
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,560
And flush.

70
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:53,340
Why does the Champion 4 prevail where some other toilets can fail?

71
00:04:53,341 --> 00:04:57,340
This is probably the flush valve in the toilet that you have now.

72
00:04:57,341 --> 00:04:59,340
It's roughly two inches in diameter.

73
00:04:59,341 --> 00:05:04,340
A high-performance toilet may have a flush valve that's three inches in diameter.

74
00:05:04,341 --> 00:05:09,340
And the Champion 4 has this four-inch diameter accelerator flush valve.

75
00:05:09,341 --> 00:05:11,340
You can see it's very large.

76
00:05:11,341 --> 00:05:14,340
It allows the water to leave the tank at a very rapid rate.

77
00:05:14,341 --> 00:05:21,120
rapid rate. The other difference with the Champion 4 is that the trapway is 2 3 8

78
00:05:21,121 --> 00:05:27,120
inches in diameter, which is the largest that you can purchase. Most toilets have

79
00:05:27,121 --> 00:05:33,220
just a 2 inch trapway. That extra 3 8 of an inch can make all the difference

80
00:05:33,221 --> 00:05:42,960
between a flushing success and a flushing failure. When you trip the lever, 1.6

81
00:05:42,961 --> 00:05:46,800
gallons of water exit through the piston action flush valve in under a second.

82
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:52,603
The rest of the sequence is generally similar with all toilets.

83
00:05:52,604 --> 00:05:54,080
Some of the water travels

84
00:05:54,081 --> 00:05:55,959
through the rim into the bowl.

85
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:59,780
The remaining water goes down a channel and into the trapway.

86
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:05,000
A siphon effect combined with gravity pulls everything down the drain.

87
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:12,040
Thanks to a set of water usage regulations passed by the Environmental Protection Agency

88
00:06:12,041 --> 00:06:20,280
in 1992, the Champion 4 and all newer toilets have only 1.6 gallons per flush with which

89
00:06:20,281 --> 00:06:20,981
