sicknesses, allows people perfect hygiene by being able to brush their teeth more often.

139
00:09:08,220 --> 00:09:11,980
It's a very simple thing, but do without your sink for a while and see where you are.

140
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:19,300
At the Kohler Company in Kohler, Wisconsin, man and machine join forces to produce the

141
00:09:19,301 --> 00:09:21,400
simple yet indispensable sink.

142
00:09:21,401 --> 00:09:30,280
Here, your sink starts off as a pile of manhole covers, car parts, dumbbells, and other scrap iron.

143
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:36,640
A powerful magnet carries the scrap to a furnace, heated to 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit.

144
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:43,420
Workers slag off the impurities in preparation for the big pour.

145
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:51,400
Meanwhile, the sink molds, made from sand mixed with water

146
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:55,400
and chemical binders, wait on the other side of the factory.

147
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:57,400
During the molding process, you actually

148
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,400
have two halves of a mold.

149
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,400
When we put them together, there's

150
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:03,400
about a quarter of an inch in between,

151
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:06,340
and that's what fills with the iron.

152
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:09,420
The melt is brought over by forklift

153
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:11,340
and poured into the mold.

154
00:10:11,341 --> 00:10:15,340
forklift and poured into the molds.

155
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:20,340
It takes just a minute for the molten metal to solidify,

156
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:23,380
and another couple of hours for the sinks to cool enough to be

157
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:27,280
sent for grinding and cleaning.

158
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:30,300
We're doing that robotically.

159
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:35,300
Then it's going and actually clearing out the drain hole

160
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:37,340
and the supply hole.

161
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:40,840
Once that's complete, it sets it back off on the conveyor

162
00:10:40,841 --> 00:10:44,340
conveyor over here, where it comes to the grinder,

163
00:10:44,341 --> 00:10:47,080
it will manually remove the remaining clashing

164
00:10:47,081 --> 00:10:49,840
and any high iron on the piece prior to getting it

165
00:10:49,841 --> 00:10:52,880
ready for enameling.

166
00:10:52,881 --> 00:10:55,320
Sprayers apply a coat of pink slurry

167
00:10:55,321 --> 00:10:57,500
that will act as a primer to help the enamel adhere

168
00:10:57,501 --> 00:11:00,800
to the iron.

169
00:11:00,801 --> 00:11:04,500
Then it's into an oven for eight minutes.

170
00:11:04,501 --> 00:11:06,560
Now the casting has been reheated up

171
00:11:06,561 --> 00:11:09,160
to approximately 1,700 degrees.

172
00:11:09,161 --> 00:11:11,560
out of the furnace and then bring it over here

173
00:11:11,660 --> 00:11:14,140
and place it in the enameling frame where they'll apply

174
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:16,260
the first coat of enamel glass.

175
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:17,900
That's in a powder format.

176
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,540
It's important to get as much on as quick as you can

177
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:22,540
when the casting's the hottest.

178
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:25,540
So they'll complete this first coat right now,

179
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:27,240
and then it goes back into the furnace

180
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:30,140
where they'll melt that in.

181
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:33,080
One more cycle and this bath lab is ready to go.

182
00:11:33,081 --> 00:11:33,980
ago.

183
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:41,520
But the lab is just part of the sink equation.

184
00:11:41,521 --> 00:11:44,560
The obligatory faucet begins life in another foundry just

185
00:11:44,561 --> 00:11:46,520
down the street.

186
00:11:46,521 --> 00:11:51,340
Only here, they're melting brass at 1,800 degrees.

187
00:11:51,341 --> 00:11:56,100
A forklift carries the molten metal to the casting area,

188
00:11:56,101 --> 00:11:58,900
where it'll start to take shape inside a metal mold

189
00:11:58,901 --> 00:12:01,540
with a sand core.

190
00:12:01,541 --> 00:12:04,880
At this point, he's putting the sand core into the die.

191
00:12:04,881 --> 00:12:08,780
Now he's getting his liquid metal, and he's going to pour it.

192
00:12:08,781 --> 00:12:11,280
He steps on the pedal.

193
00:12:11,281 --> 00:12:12,620
The die rotates.

194
00:12:12,621 --> 00:12:16,280
The metal flows into the die with gravity.

195
00:12:16,281 --> 00:12:17,920
Right now, it is solidifying.

196
00:12:17,921 --> 00:12:20,460
It takes about 20 seconds to solidify.

197
00:12:20,461 --> 00:12:23,960
At that point, he takes it out, puts it in a car to cool,

198
00:12:23,961 --> 00:12:27,740
and it'll then look like this.

199
00:12:27,741 --> 00:12:30,860
Then the faucet is machined,

200
00:12:30,861 --> 00:12:34,060
polished and prepped for plating.

201
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:42,300
In the plating area, this giant dipping rig filled with various acids, cleaning agents,

202
00:12:42,301 --> 00:12:47,580
and chemical compounds ensures your faucet's metalwork doesn't become a pile of rust

203
00:12:47,581 --> 00:12:50,300
from oxidation and corrosive hard water deposits.

204
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:57,500
The entire plating operation, lending the faucets their protective layers, takes about 90 minutes.

205
00:12:57,501 --> 00:13:02,500
Then Kohler has to make them look nice.

206
00:13:02,501 --> 00:13:07,500
This happens in the PVD room, where metallurgy meets chemistry.

207
00:13:07,501 --> 00:13:12,500
In physical vapor deposition, over the top of the chrome layer,

208
00:13:12,501 --> 00:13:15,500
we put a, usually it's zirconium nitrite,

209
00:13:15,501 --> 00:13:20,500
titanium nitrite coating over the top to give us the different

210
00:13:20,501 --> 00:13:22,500
colors that Kohler sells.

211
00:13:22,501 --> 00:13:26,500
The pieces are preheated.

212
00:13:26,501 --> 00:13:30,520
preheated and then placed inside this vacuum chamber

213
00:13:30,521 --> 00:13:35,480
for 90 minutes, where they'll be bombarded with metallic ions

214
00:13:35,481 --> 00:13:39,860
with the help of turbo-molecular pumps.

215
00:13:39,861 --> 00:13:42,620
This electronic coating process will determine the faucet's

216
00:13:42,621 --> 00:13:47,060
finish, everything from vibrant brushed bronze

217
00:13:47,061 --> 00:13:51,400
to polished chrome to brushed nickel.

218
00:13:51,401 --> 00:13:53,960
Then the faucets are off to this area,

219
00:13:53,961 --> 00:13:56,940
where spouts and handles of all imaginable designs

220
00:13:56,941 --> 00:14:01,120
are assembled, tested, and packaged.

221
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:06,300
In today's marketplace,

222
