00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:57,700
Steam showers originally were used as a therapy.

251
00:15:57,701 --> 00:16:00,740
Today, they're a luxury that are used in some of the finer

252
00:16:00,741 --> 00:16:01,640
homes.

253
00:16:01,641 --> 00:16:02,860
They're electric.

254
00:16:02,861 --> 00:16:05,940
Water falls down on a hot coil or stainless steel plate.

255
00:16:05,941 --> 00:16:07,040
The steam is created.

256
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:14,720
The shower also has a little something

257
00:16:14,721 --> 00:16:17,580
for the other senses.

258
00:16:17,581 --> 00:16:19,720
What you see behind me is the ambient rain panel.

259
00:16:19,721 --> 00:16:23,420
which is a 21-inch LED lit panel.

260
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:25,660
It also incorporates audio.

261
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:28,560
You can hook up your computer or any MP3 player to it

262
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:30,260
and take any of those files and actually play them

263
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:33,460
in the shower through our waterproof speakers.

264
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:36,780
It's like having a private disco in your shower.

265
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:38,180
And there's more.

266
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:40,180
But maybe the coolest feature is the different effects

267
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:41,680
that we have.

268
00:16:41,780 --> 00:16:43,680
Let's say you've always wanted a skylight in your bathroom.

269
00:16:43,780 --> 00:16:45,680
You can pick our sunny clouds feature and actually have moving

270
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:46,680
clouds in your ceiling.

271
00:16:49,720 --> 00:16:54,760
Mix and match the options to reflect or enhance your mood.

272
00:16:54,761 --> 00:16:56,740
The shower's entire symphony of functions

273
00:16:56,741 --> 00:17:00,340
is conducted by the remote user interface that can save

274
00:17:00,341 --> 00:17:02,380
and instantly recall personal settings.

275
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:10,520
Because some of the DTV2's parts are delicate,

276
00:17:10,521 --> 00:17:13,680
workers must hand assemble and inspect them individually.

277
00:17:13,681 --> 00:17:22,020
And needless to say, installing the DTV2 is a bit more complicated than your run-of-the-mill

278
00:17:22,021 --> 00:17:23,020
shower.

279
00:17:23,021 --> 00:17:27,080
The water delivery portion is going to have a different valve installation behind

280
00:17:27,081 --> 00:17:29,020
the wall, but your plumber can easily do that.

281
00:17:29,021 --> 00:17:31,860
Where it's going to be different is you have to include an electrician for some parts

282
00:17:31,861 --> 00:17:35,940
of the system, such as the lighting, the audio, and some of the pieces behind the wall.

283
00:17:35,941 --> 00:17:39,880
You also might need to include an integrator, someone who helps with setting up home networks

284
00:17:39,881 --> 00:17:42,760
to help you with the audio piece.

285
00:17:42,761 --> 00:17:45,500
The whole shebang, hydrotherapy, chroma therapy,

286
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:48,000
and all the rest, will probably run you somewhere

287
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:52,100
in the area of $10,000.

288
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:56,500
But it still might be cheaper than a future of actual therapy.

289
00:17:56,600 --> 00:17:57,900
I think the future of the home shower

290
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,420
is basically personalizing your shower.

291
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:02,020
What you see here is personalization

292
00:18:02,120 --> 00:18:05,320
on a pretty big level.

293
00:18:05,420 --> 00:18:08,920
Of course, to know where the shower is going,

294
00:18:09,020 --> 00:18:11,560
it helps to know where it's been.

295
00:18:11,561 --> 00:18:14,860
It's documented in vases and murals, places that we see

296
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:17,700
drawings of ancient Greeks that showered.

297
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:19,460
They showered not for hygiene.

298
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:23,500
They believed it energized them and gave them power.

299
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:26,040
Cold water flowed via terracotta pipes

300
00:18:26,140 --> 00:18:28,280
and over the bathers through shower heads shaped

301
00:18:28,380 --> 00:18:29,240
like animal faces.

302
00:18:32,380 --> 00:18:35,180
The ancient Romans and many generations thereafter

303
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:36,820
preferred their baths to showering.

304
00:18:36,821 --> 00:18:40,120
and the water pouring.

305
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:42,960
Rudimentary shower contraptions began to resurface

306
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:47,540
in the early 1800s, like this one,

307
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:52,100
called the American Virginia Stool Shower.

308
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:55,400
The bather placed the wood machine inside a bathtub

309
00:18:55,500 --> 00:18:59,000
and operated a hand pump to bring water up a hose

310
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:01,580
and out over his head.

311
00:19:01,680 --> 00:19:04,520
A foot pedal maneuvered an attached scrub brush up

312
00:19:04,521 --> 00:19:10,135
down as needed. For decades, the shower remained a novelty.

313
00:19:10,136 --> 00:19:13,180
Only when indoor plumbing became

314
00:19:13,181 --> 00:19:19,984
more commonplace in the mid to late 19th century could showers become practical.

315
00:19:19,985 --> 00:19:20,920
Still, well

316
00:19:20,921 --> 00:19:26,720
into the 20th century, most Americans bathed in tubs, but only about once or twice a week.

317
00:19:28,060 --> 00:19:33,660
During the post-World War II tract housing boom, builders incorporated showers into most bathrooms.

318
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:37,360
making it easier for people to begin a transition

319
00:19:37,460 --> 00:19:41,260
to a more frequent washing routine.

320
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:44,260
The major shift between the bathtub and the shower

321
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:46,360
came in the 1970s.

322
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:49,360
It came about because people were in a larger hurry,

323
00:19:49,460 --> 00:19:51,600
wanted to get ready quicker, and were more

324
00:19:51,700 --> 00:19:54,780
interested in cleanliness.

325
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:57,180
To accommodate new, larger conveniences

326
00:19:57,280 --> 00:19:58,680
like standalone showers.

327
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:03,920
Today's average master bathroom has roughly

328
00:20:03,921 --> 00:20:10,940
roughly doubled in size since the 1950s. But space and price tag aside, a luxury

329
00:20:10,941 --> 00:20:14,060
shower can carry another cost if you're not careful.

330
00:20:14,061 --> 00:20:19,820
Large rain head or multiple side sprays can use on average between 10 and 20

331
00:20:19,821 --> 00:20:25,280
gallons of water per minute. Most of us still have one of these, a standard

332
00:20:25,281 --> 00:20:32,000
showerhead. Showerheads produced prior to the EPA Act of 1992 expelled about

333
00:20:32,001 --> 00:20:34,154
gallons of water per minute.

334
00:20:34,155 --> 00:20:39,000
Today, regulations have reduced that to 2.5 gallons per minute.

335
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,624
But which uses more water? Bathing in a tub?

336
00:20:46,625 --> 00:20:50,000
Or showering with a standard shower head?

337
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:58,000
A shower head that uses 2.5 gallons of minute will use 25 gallons over a 10-minute period,

338
00:20:58,001 --> 00:21:01,240
where the average tub holds about 50 gallons of water.

339
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:06,120
So a 10-minute shower would use roughly half as much water as a bathing in the tub.

340
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:11,760
American Standard has put a new spin on low flow,

341
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:14,640
with a FlowWise version of the showerhead.

342
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:19,960
The FlowWise uses just 1.5 gallons per minute,

343
00:21:20,340 --> 00:21:23,400
but the bather is none the wiser and none the dirtier.

344
00:21:23,401 --> 00:21:27,000
A conventional 2.5 gallons per minute showerhead

345
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:29,100
does little more than deliver a metered amount

346
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:31,640
of pressurized water through small holes.

347
00:21:33,780 --> 00:21:37,480
The Flow-wise showerhead incorporates a small turbine

348
00:21:37,580 --> 00:21:39,980
located in the center.

349
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:42,920
The pressurized water makes it spin.

350
00:21:43,020 --> 00:21:44,680
The turbine breaks up the droplets

351
00:21:44,780 --> 00:21:48,100
while infusing them with air as they're pushed out.

352
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:53,400
The air makes less water seem like the same amount.

353
00:21:53,401 --> 00:21:55,260
so that at gallon and a half a minute,

354
00:21:55,261 --> 00:21:57,800
you get a nice, forceful shower.

355
00:21:59,640 --> 00:22:01,440
As a compromise, American Standard

356
00:22:01,441 --> 00:22:04,520
is also incorporated an element of personal choice.

357
00:22:04,521 --> 00:22:07,380
If you're taking a shower and you're all soaked up,

358
00:22:07,381 --> 00:22:09,380
have shampoo in your hair, and you want to rinse,

359
00:22:11,220 --> 00:22:12,720
you just convert the shower

360
00:22:12,721 --> 00:22:14,860
to a maximum of two gallons a minute.

361
00:22:14,861 --> 00:22:17,960
It's a combination of the turban and an outer spray pattern.

362
00:22:18,960 --> 00:22:21,460
And this way you get a good, satisfying rinse.

363
00:22:21,461 --> 00:22:24,560
The great thing about FlowWise is when you turn it off,

364
00:22:25,560 --> 00:22:28,860
it automatically resets itself to a gallon and a half.

365
00:22:29,320 --> 00:22:31,180
This way, you're always conserving water,

366
00:22:31,480 --> 00:22:33,260
and you have to choose to use more.

367
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:37,340
Before the FlowWise and their other products reach your bathroom,

368
00:22:38,220 --> 00:22:42,020
American Standards Design Team puts them through demanding life cycle tests.

369
00:22:43,580 --> 00:22:45,140
This is our FlowWise showerhead,

370
00:22:45,141 --> 00:22:47,780
and what we're doing right now on this reliability test route

371
00:22:47,781 --> 00:22:51,480
is making sure that the two different settings work properly over time.

372
00:22:51,980 --> 00:22:55,280
This reliability test rate runs 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

373
00:22:56,140 --> 00:22:59,460
We start the cycle going, and computers run all of this.

374
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:03,680
And at the end of the day and over the weekend, we turn off the lights and go home,

375
00:23:03,681 --> 00:23:09,340
but the test continues so that we can get the maximum length of test in in the shortest amount of time.

376
00:23:10,220 --> 00:23:13,360
We know what you're thinking, and no, they're not being hypocrites.

377
00:23:13,361 --> 00:23:16,360
The water that it uses goes through the fitting,

378
00:23:16,460 --> 00:23:19,660
goes into our collection pipes, goes through these pipes,

379
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:22,700
back into our treatment area where it's reprocessed,

380
00:23:22,800 --> 00:23:26,100
rechilled, and repressurized so that we can use it again.

381
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:28,600
In this way, our impact as far as water usage

382
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:31,020
is relatively little.

383
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:33,720
It doesn't matter if your shower is tricked out

384
00:23:33,820 --> 00:23:35,020
or low flow.

385
00:23:35,120 --> 00:23:38,160
Washing and water alone won't do the job.

386
00:23:38,260 --> 00:23:41,520
Do everyone around you a favor and look into some other

387
00:23:41,521 --> 00:23:43,320
to some other bathroom offerings.

388
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:49,400
At the turn of the 17th century, Queen Elizabeth I

389
00:23:49,401 --> 00:23:53,040
boasted that she took a bath once a month.

390
00:23:53,041 --> 00:23:56,420
Whether I need it or not, Bathroom Tech 2

391
00:23:56,421 --> 00:23:58,660
will return on Modern Marvels.

392
00:24:02,560 --> 00:24:04,320
So you're wet.

393
00:24:04,321 --> 00:24:07,460
But what's going to get all that dirt off?

394
00:24:07,461 --> 00:24:08,860
Let's ask her.

395
00:24:08,861 --> 00:24:11,460
She looks pretty clean.

396
00:24:11,461 --> 00:24:13,860
Meet Cara Dwin Hershberger of Elgin, Texas.

397
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:16,720
Cara Dwin is a fourth-generation soap maker,

398
00:24:17,340 --> 00:24:19,680
catering to those who appreciate craft and tradition.

399
00:24:20,920 --> 00:24:24,520
Soap is a basic cleansing agent that can act as a bridge

400
00:24:24,521 --> 00:24:27,420
between oil and dirt and water.

401
00:24:29,100 --> 00:24:31,140
Soap essentially acts as an emulsifier

402
00:24:31,141 --> 00:24:34,120
or something that facilitates the blending of substances

403
00:24:34,121 --> 00:24:35,800
that don't normally mix.

404
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:40,400
Oil and dirt on the skin won't combine with water.

405
00:24:41,460 --> 00:24:45,060
The emulsifying soap helps disperse water into the oil,

406
00:24:45,061 --> 00:24:47,360
while detaching it from the surface.

407
00:24:49,100 --> 00:24:51,400
The oil and dirt are suspended and diluted,

408
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:53,100
so they can be washed away.

409
00:24:55,100 --> 00:24:57,020
Caridwin makes soap the old-fashioned way,

410
00:24:57,240 --> 00:25:00,220
like they did from the 14th to mid-19th century,

411
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:02,800
by hand, in a cauldron.

412
00:25:03,740 --> 00:25:05,700
The most basic components of soap making

413
00:25:05,701 --> 00:25:08,840
are some form of grease or fat and lye.

414
00:25:08,841 --> 00:25:15,840
Lye can now be synthetically produced or it can be dripped from various types of wood ashes or seaweed ashes.

415
00:25:15,841 --> 00:25:21,840
Some experts attribute great historical significance to the humble soap recipe.

416
00:25:21,841 --> 00:25:27,840
When soap making became more popular in the 1300s, midwives began washing their hands.

417
00:25:27,841 --> 00:25:33,840
And with midwives washing their hands, it led to a decrease in infant mortality rates.

418
00:25:33,841 --> 00:25:36,840
So there's certain schools of thought amongst anthropologists

419
00:25:36,841 --> 00:25:40,840
that actually soap making helped bring about the Renaissance

420
00:25:40,841 --> 00:25:42,840
by increasing the middle class.

421
00:25:42,841 --> 00:25:46,840
Caridwin kicks off the soap making process by chopping and melting

422
00:25:46,841 --> 00:25:50,840
the rich fat taken from the kidney region of a steer.

423
00:25:50,841 --> 00:25:54,840
I am rendering down the beef tallow, so I'll have a pure fat,

424
00:25:54,841 --> 00:25:58,840
as opposed to a fat with a lot of connective tissue,

425
00:25:58,841 --> 00:26:01,840
meat, blood, things like that.

426
00:26:01,841 --> 00:26:06,340
In the cauldron, she combines the tallow with pork lard.

427
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:12,940
Once the fat is melted, she adds in the other active ingredient, alkali.

428
00:26:14,020 --> 00:26:18,140
I have a 50% sodium hydroxide or lye water solution,

429
00:26:18,640 --> 00:26:21,400
and I'll be adding the lye water to the fats.

430
00:26:21,480 --> 00:26:23,640
The fats are at this point at about 100 degrees.

431
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:26,600
Too much lye can be a bad thing.

432
00:26:27,900 --> 00:26:29,560
Alkali is caustic.

433
00:26:29,561 --> 00:26:32,080
It can cause minor burns to the skin.

434
00:26:32,620 --> 00:26:34,980
Nothing spectacular like we saw in Fight Club,

435
00:26:35,120 --> 00:26:36,700
but it will cause a minor burn.

436
00:26:37,340 --> 00:26:40,520
And then she stirs and stirs.

437
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:43,700
This is actually activating the saponification

438
00:26:43,701 --> 00:26:45,820
during which the fatty acids and the oils

439
00:26:45,821 --> 00:26:49,720
and the caustic begin to transform into soap.

440
00:26:51,080 --> 00:26:52,820
It'll actually start to take on the look

441
00:26:52,821 --> 00:26:55,580
of a thick cream gravy or a hot cooked pudding.

442
00:26:56,580 --> 00:26:59,240
To test the lye level in a batch of soap brew,

443
00:26:59,560 --> 00:27:02,760
Caridwin employs a most sophisticated method.

444
00:27:02,761 --> 00:27:05,820
One of the most traditional methods is to taste it.

445
00:27:06,360 --> 00:27:07,660
I'm really not kidding.

446
00:27:08,560 --> 00:27:10,000
I get a little bit on my finger.

447
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:17,920
And that is almost ready.

448
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:23,300
When the batch is fully cooked, she scoops the batter into a shallow mold,

449
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:27,560
where it'll cool and harden into an alternative to mass-produced soaps.

450
00:27:29,560 --> 00:27:33,960
A lot of what sets my soaps apart is what I don't use.

451
00:27:34,060 --> 00:27:36,960
I don't use a lot of the more modern synthetic ingredients

452
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:39,400
because I simply don't feel like they're necessary

453
00:27:39,500 --> 00:27:41,500
to make a good soap.

454
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:43,200
And I really don't feel like they're necessary

455
00:27:43,300 --> 00:27:44,300
to put on one's skin.

456
00:27:47,020 --> 00:27:49,720
Mass produced or handmade,

457
00:27:49,820 --> 00:27:52,220
soap is just the first line of defense

458
00:27:52,320 --> 00:27:55,620
against smelling offensive.

459
00:27:55,621 --> 00:27:59,360
But for some, B.O. is a formidable foe.

460
00:28:00,740 --> 00:28:03,300
Body odor is primarily generated in the armpits

461
00:28:03,301 --> 00:28:05,620
by a concentration of sweat glands.

462
00:28:06,680 --> 00:28:09,260
Moisture secreted by these glands is initially odorless,

463
00:28:09,261 --> 00:28:12,540
but the natural lipids, or fats it contains,

464
00:28:13,080 --> 00:28:14,840
provide a growth medium for bacteria.

465
00:28:16,180 --> 00:28:18,540
These bacteria interact with the lipids,

466
00:28:19,060 --> 00:28:21,940
converting them into compounds like isovaleric acid

467
00:28:21,941 --> 00:28:24,620
that give sweat that characteristic smell.

468
00:28:25,620 --> 00:28:30,020
For centuries, people wanted some way to control body odor.

469
00:28:30,220 --> 00:28:33,120
They used different types of perfumes, colognes,

470
00:28:33,260 --> 00:28:35,300
before the bath became commonplace.

471
00:28:35,620 --> 00:28:39,260
But around 1888, the first trademark deodorant came out.

472
00:28:40,260 --> 00:28:43,180
Some of the early products masked bad smells,

473
00:28:43,480 --> 00:28:44,720
but caused irritation.

474
00:28:46,640 --> 00:28:49,980
Fortunately for modern noses, these products have evolved,

475
00:28:50,460 --> 00:28:52,460
so even those with the most sensitive skin

476
00:28:52,461 --> 00:28:53,480
can protect themselves.

477
00:28:55,620 --> 00:28:59,680
So which to choose, deodorant or antiperspirant?

478
00:29:00,640 --> 00:29:03,080
Deodorants mask odor and kill bacteria,

479
00:29:03,220 --> 00:29:04,480
but do not reduce wetness.

480
00:29:05,260 --> 00:29:07,560
Antiperspirants reduce wetness

481
00:29:07,561 --> 00:29:09,800
through inhibiting the production of sweat.

482
00:29:10,980 --> 00:29:13,340
The technology controls odor three ways.

483
00:29:14,760 --> 00:29:18,640
One, the active ingredient eliminates odor-causing bacteria.

484
00:29:18,900 --> 00:29:21,020
Two, the fragrance masks the odor.

485
00:29:21,520 --> 00:29:23,380
Three, the reduction in moisture

486
00:29:23,381 --> 00:29:26,540
reduces the amount of bacteria that's able to grow.

487
00:29:27,980 --> 00:29:29,820
Once antiperspirant is applied,

488
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:32,880
the active ingredient, an aluminum compound,

489
00:29:33,640 --> 00:29:36,820
reacts with moisture on the skin and becomes gelatinous.

490
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:41,400
The solution plugs the pores, inhibiting sweat secretion.

491
00:29:42,780 --> 00:29:44,920
Regardless of which type of product you use

492
00:29:44,921 --> 00:29:46,260
to keep yourself inoffensive,

493
00:29:47,080 --> 00:29:49,760
there's a good chance it came from Unilever.

494
00:29:50,580 --> 00:29:52,180
In Rayford, North Carolina,

495
00:29:52,181 --> 00:29:55,780
makers of Degree and other personal care products.

496
00:29:57,360 --> 00:30:02,100
This is where our liquid unloading takes place at the Rafer factory for the antiperspirant production.

497
00:30:02,980 --> 00:30:08,400
We unload our emollients in liquid form from bulk tank trucks into these bulk tanks.

498
00:30:08,480 --> 00:30:14,120
They're about 100,000-pound tanks, and it can be pumped at 2 tons per minute directly into our compounding department.

499
00:30:15,180 --> 00:30:21,540
State-of-the-art mixing equipment combines prescribed amounts of liquid ingredients, fragrance, and aluminum compounds.

500
00:30:22,180 --> 00:30:27,180
heated to 158 degrees Fahrenheit.

